WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.639 --> 00:00:04.608
- I don't know how not to be creative.

00:00:04.608 --> 00:00:07.390
It's something that just, it just like
runs through my blood

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.014
just like being Korean does.

00:00:10.014 --> 00:00:12.373
It's never been enough
for me to just make Hanji.

00:00:12.373 --> 00:00:14.295
I'm an artist.

00:00:14.295 --> 00:00:17.462
(bright string music)

00:00:21.285 --> 00:00:25.035
My name is Aimee Lee, and
my life mission is to

00:00:25.035 --> 00:00:28.368
make and promote Hanji across the world.

00:00:29.746 --> 00:00:32.753
Hanji is, as an umbrella
term, just Korean paper,

00:00:32.753 --> 00:00:35.284
but the kind of Hanji
that I'm interested in

00:00:35.284 --> 00:00:39.094
is made according to the
tradition and made by hand

00:00:39.094 --> 00:00:40.927
and made from scratch.

00:00:41.963 --> 00:00:45.202
I was born in New York City
to Korean immigrant parents

00:00:45.202 --> 00:00:48.369
who taught me Korean in the household.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.917
Growing up, I loved making
music and so, I always had that

00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:56.080
proclivity towards the arts.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.868
But I didn't know anything
about paper making

00:00:57.868 --> 00:01:01.015
until I went to graduate
school for book arts.

00:01:01.015 --> 00:01:03.641
When I was looking for
information on Hanji,

00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:06.494
there was very little,
at least in English,

00:01:06.494 --> 00:01:08.446
so it became very clear that
if I wanted to learn more

00:01:08.446 --> 00:01:10.411
about Hanji, I would have
to go directly to Korea.

00:01:10.411 --> 00:01:12.580
(soft music)

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.989
The paper mulberry tree
that grows in Korea

00:01:14.989 --> 00:01:18.274
has to withstand the Korean monsoon season

00:01:18.274 --> 00:01:20.577
where it's really hot
and humid in the summer,

00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:24.385
and then these intense,
snowy cold winters.

00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:28.753
So this tree is incredibly
resilient and really strong.

00:01:28.753 --> 00:01:32.265
Not only does that tree have
to withstand that climate,

00:01:32.265 --> 00:01:35.861
the people do too, so
bringing those two things

00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:39.936
together creates a product
that's totally different from

00:01:39.936 --> 00:01:41.269
any other place.

00:01:43.321 --> 00:01:46.238
(foreign speaking)

00:01:58.691 --> 00:02:02.258
Traditionally, Hanji is made by men.

00:02:02.258 --> 00:02:05.202
And a lot of them just took
one look at me and said,

00:02:05.202 --> 00:02:06.212
"You're a woman.

00:02:06.212 --> 00:02:07.737
"You can't do this," or they would say,

00:02:07.737 --> 00:02:09.797
"You're not strong enough."

00:02:09.797 --> 00:02:12.862
That was just so far out of what they knew

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:17.029
that they didn't believe
me until they saw me work.

00:02:18.348 --> 00:02:21.265
(foreign speaking)

00:02:34.071 --> 00:02:36.226
I'd never thought about
it because it's just

00:02:36.226 --> 00:02:40.680
what I've been driven to do,
but I know from the outside,

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.282
it looks like I have a lot
of audacity for trying to

00:02:44.282 --> 00:02:48.092
do something that only
men traditionally do,

00:02:48.092 --> 00:02:50.092
and even to this day do.

00:02:51.428 --> 00:02:54.345
(foreign speaking)

00:03:03.899 --> 00:03:07.294
When I came back from Korea,
I was doing all this weaving,

00:03:07.294 --> 00:03:10.602
which is a Korean
tradition called Jiseung.

00:03:10.602 --> 00:03:13.551
You have to cut big sheets
of paper into narrow strips

00:03:13.551 --> 00:03:17.506
and then you have to make cords
and then out of these cords,

00:03:17.506 --> 00:03:20.256
you can then weave like basketry.

00:03:22.851 --> 00:03:25.768
(foreign speaking)

00:03:28.495 --> 00:03:31.566
I think that the way that
you keep tradition alive

00:03:31.566 --> 00:03:33.687
is to just keep evolving it.

00:03:33.687 --> 00:03:36.531
The most exciting work I've
been doing with the weaving

00:03:36.531 --> 00:03:39.035
is making ducks, and those are based on

00:03:39.035 --> 00:03:42.158
Korean wedding ducks that
are given to bride and groom

00:03:42.158 --> 00:03:45.658
to promote marital fidelity and fertility.

00:03:46.881 --> 00:03:50.172
People were very surprised
when I came to them saying,

00:03:50.172 --> 00:03:52.498
"Oh, I wanna learn how to make Hanji,"

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:56.112
but you have to know when
to walk away and you also

00:03:56.112 --> 00:03:58.249
have to know when to
recognize that someone

00:03:58.249 --> 00:04:00.868
may be able to give you
a chance, even if you are

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.618
a very strange bird.

